    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d223">

                <pb facs="0328" n="226" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg226"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/6007: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-03-08T22:00:00+08:00">March 8, 1933—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 9—12:23 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>225. Chiang Kai-shek is expected in Paotingfu whither Chang Hsueh-liang is
                    expected to go for a conference with him. T. V. Soong is also understood to be
                    expected here in the North to attend conference.</p>
                <p>Chinese appear still to hold Kupeikou and Western marches of Jehol where fighting
                    has been going on. Spectacular collapse of Chinese defensive in Jehol is
                    producing feeling of resentment against above-named leaders who are now on the
                    horns of a dilemma. This leadership is, I believe, opposed to any attempt to
                    dislodge Japanese from Jehol but public resentment may make them feel it
                    necessary to take some such action. If they take action against Jehol Japanese
                    will without doubt invade this area. If they do not take some such action
                    popular resentment may succeed in disrupting Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
